Since $\mathrm{f}(x)$ is continuous in $[-1,1]$, it is continuous at $x=0$. $\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad \lim _{x \rightarrow 0^{-}} \mathrm{f}(x)=\lim _{x \rightarrow 0^{+}} \mathrm{f}(x) \\ \text{$ and $} \Rightarrow \lim _{x \rightarrow 0} \frac{\sqrt{1+\mathrm{m} x}-\sqrt{1-\mathrm{m} x}}{x}=\lim _{x \rightarrow 0} \frac{2 x+1}{x-2}\\ \text{ and } \Rightarrow \lim _{x \rightarrow 0} \frac{(1+\mathrm{m} x-1+\mathrm{m} x)}{x(\sqrt{1+\mathrm{m} x}+\sqrt{1-\mathrm{m} x})}=\frac{2(0)+1}{0-2}\\ \text{ and } \Rightarrow \frac{2 \mathrm{ m}}{1+1}=\frac{1}{-2}\\ \text{ and } \Rightarrow \mathrm{m}=\frac{-1}{2}
\end{aligned}$

